AU  - Seidel, M
AU  - Langheinrich, U
AU  - Luderitz, V
AU  - Speierl, T
T2  - WASSERWIRTSCHAFT
AB  - The freshwater pearl mussel (Margaritifera margaritifera) is an indicator, keystone, target and flagship species in aquatic ecology and nature conservation. In Europe, its populations decrease on markedly. There are a lot of results concerning the biology and, in detail, the autecology of this species but there is a deficit in information about the accompanying fauna and flora in pearl mussel streams. This contribution deals with the fish and macroinvertebrate fauna and with the aquatic vegetation of each two such streams in the Bavarian Fichtelgebirge and in the Luneburger Heide, Lower Saxony. The evaluation is done with the recent assessment procedures. The results show that intact and reproducing populations of mussels are linked with a very good ecological status of the water body. In the river Lutter as one of the few German streams with growing pearl mussel populations and in the stream Lachte with "mussel expectation" an unique clear-water invertebrate biocoenosis that refuges several red list species was found. The stocks of brown trout (Salmo trutta) that is the host fish of pearl mussels is several times higher in the Lutter and in the Lachte than in the Zinnbach and in the Sudliche Regnitz. The used methodical approach can help to assess the chance of success for projects of mussel return.
