TI  - Hydromorphological indicators for the ecological status of fish inhabitating the lower trout region in streams in the North-German lowlands
AU  - Brunke, M
T2  - HYDROLOGIE UND WASSERBEWIRTSCHAFTUNG
AB  - The fish fauna of many lowland streams does not achieve the good ecological status owing to a complex of various historical and present stressors. Hydromorphological degradations are frequently considered to exert negative impacts on the ecological integrity in particular for fish. In this paper, statistical relationships between fish assemblages in the lower trout region and hydromorphological variables in lowland streams of Schleswig-Holstein, Germany, are analysed.
Several variables of channel form, adjacent landuse, instream structures, and bottom substrates indicated hydromorphological degradation and proved to influence fish assemblages. Riparian softwood forests and large variability in water depth were positive structural indicators of biological integrity in fish. Negative indicators comprised intensive use as grassland, a high portion of mud in bottom substrates, riverbed incision, excessively widened channel profiles, and excessive portions of sand in substrates of smaller streams. Species richness correlated significantly with habitat volume, but not with area and length of the sampled stretches. The assessment of fish assemblages by the German fish-biological classification system (fiBS) rendered plausible results that were in a complex relationship with hydromorphological conditions. The moderate quality class was most variable in terms of fish assemblage composition and morphological degradations. Finally, recommendations for restoration measures are given based on the findings of this analysis.
