TI  - A concept for the support of potamodromous fish species in impounded rivers of the Danube catchment using the example of the middle river Iller
AU  - Epple, T
AU  - Born, O
AU  - Wetzel, KF
AU  - Friedmann, A
T2  - HYDROLOGIE UND WASSERBEWIRTSCHAFTUNG
AB  - At the river Iller between Altusried and Lautrach (Bavaria, Germany), the river continuity at five hydroelectric power plants was re-established with natural fish-bypass channels and gravel structures were restored in three power plant underwater sections. Between 2015 and 2020, the migration of grayling (Thymallus thymallus), brown trout (Salmo trutta fario), Danube salmon (Hucho hucho), barbel (Barbus barbus) and nase (Chondrostoma nasus) through the fish-bypass channels was investigated by means of counting basins, their stocks in the fish-bypass channels and in the main river were surveyed by electrofishing and their spawning in the fish-bypass channels was documented by optical observations. Nearly all size classes of the fish species studied used the fish-bypass channels for ascending movements. From the different size class distributions of the investigated species in the fish-bypass channels and in the main river, preferences regarding the water compartments main river and fish-bypass channel during a simplified life cycle were derived. Depending on the species, habitats in the fish-bypass channels and in the main river are used during the life cycle. In some stretches of water, nase used spawning habitats exclusively in the fish-bypass channels, while barbels spawned only in the main river. Based on the habitat preferences of the species studied, a concept for the support of potamodromous fish species was developed, which is based on three pillars and includes habitat-creating measurements in the main river and tributary and side waters such as fish-bypass channels as well as the realization of the river passability. Since the different species show partly very different habitat uses, restoration measures have to consider the requirements of the target fish species at species level. Restoration projects must not be carried out exclusively in individual water body compartments but must include the main river as well as tributary and side waters such as fish-bypass channels in order to sufficiently support populations of potamodromic fish species.
