AU  - UTSCHICK, H
AU  - AMMER, U
AU  - KOLBECK, H
T2  - FORSTWISSENSCHAFTLICHES CENTRALBLATT
AB  - ''Schachten'' are montane meadows near the Bavarian border to the CSFR, previously extensively grazed by cattle but abandoned since some 30 years ago. In 1986/87, species diversity and abundance of flowering-plant communities and bumblebees/butterflies/syrphids foraging at different flower types were investigated on three abandoned, one regularely mowed, and three more meadows, which have been cut every two years without removing the crop (mulching).
The mowed meadow showed greatest species diversity and abundance of flowers and bumblebees. On abandoned meadows, similar conditions were reached only on areas dominated by spring-water supported Agrostis tenuis communities. On drier meadows, mostly Holcis mollis displaced flowering species and the bumblebees depending on them. Butterflies were more influenced by the climatic regime than by meadow management, but single endangered species like Heodes hippothoe may benefit on abandoned, nearly flowerless meadows due to failing competition from other butterflies.
In vegetation types like Trisetetum flavescentis meadows, which contain many herbs and flowers, mulching led to good, mowing to better results in species diversity and abundance of flowers, bumblebees, butterflies, syrphids, and other insects foraging on flowers. In vegetation types dominated strongly by grass species like Agrostis tenuis or Holcus mollis, species diversity of flowering plants was lower on abandoned meadows compared to mulched ones under drier, but somewhat higher under more moist conditions. Flower abundance increased on mulched meadows, and the development of vegetative and generative parts started earlier compared to abandoned meadows. This had consequences for insect population dynamics.
Flowering species occuring mainly on small bogs, tree groups, edges (woods, roads), or in parts with rocky or decaying wooden materials are very important for foraging insects especially after mowing, mulching and in late summer/autumn (i.e. Senecio fuchsii).
Bumblebees, butterflies, syrphids and other insect groups clearly prefer different flower species and flowering types to reduce competition during the short montane summer.
Species diversity of flowers and insects is relatively small at the ''Schachten'', but many specialized species of high value for nature conservation occur, which would have become extinct without further management. Conservation by law but without appropriate management may have results strongly differing from the aims of nature conservation authorities.
