TY  - JOUR
TI  - Powerful hydropower plants in huge rivers: Requirements, synergy and limitations seen by fishery and nature conservation
AU  - Gottle, A
AU  - Schnell, J
T2  - WASSERWIRTSCHAFT
AB  - Nearly all huge rivers in Germany have been dammed during the last century for producing electricity by hydropower. As a result of the changing behaviour parameters caused by this many important ecological functions in the river systems have decreased. In the last 20 years these badly impacts get noticed more and more by the lost auf aquatic biodiversity. The government was reacting by making legislation to improve the ecological quality and to reduce man-made impacts caused by hydropower plants, e.g. by introducing the EG Water Framework Directive or Natura 2000. Relating to the impacts caused from these plants to rivers there must be differed between impacts caused by the arrangement and the operation of a plant because of different legal requirements. One example shows the protection of the fish population on hydropower plants that is ordered by law. Because of legal terms there cannot be said restoring a river, e.g. by creating a well-structured bypass channel, is a measure to protect fish from the harmful impact of the turbine. Also systemically important functions like connection of river continuum mustn't be neglected in favour of some new structure. The interchange of different stakeholders shows that there are still a lot of questions to be discussed. But different cooperation shows that there is also a high potential to improve the behaviour situation on huge power plants.
