TI  - Larus canus Common Gull
AU  - Bukaciński, D.
T2  - BWP Update
AB  - Detailed and extensive studies of the Common Gull in the last 20 years have produced a great deal of new, sometimes still unpublished, information. New data for Field characters allow better separation of juvenile individuals from other medium-sized gulls. Recent data on the status and distribution for most of the range have allowed extensive rewriting of Distribution and Population. Much new information about habitat selection of breeding and wintering birds, especially for riverine and industrial (urban) populations, are given. In new sections on Demographic parameters and Conservation, information on age of first breeding, mortality of adults, pair-bonds, hatching site fidelity, threats, and limiting factors during breeding and wintering are presented. The results of recent studies on feeding ecology, especially diets of maritime, coastal, and inland populations, and feeding behaviour are reported. The section on Movements has been rewritten to include much new material from Central Europe and data on site fidelity of wintering birds. Social pattern and behaviour and Breeding have been much extended to include new studies on reproductive ecology and behaviour (in particular on territoriality, relations within a family group, and hatching and fledging success, also from riverine populations). Data on the effects of predators, especially mink, and the impact of mass outbreaks of black flies (Simuliidae) are detailed. Many additional body measurements and weights for breeding nominate canus, as well as for all other subspecies, are given. More detail is given on the use of biometrics to sex live birds. New information is given on endoparasites. Current thinking on the taxonomy of the species is considered in Geographical variation.
