TI  - Burbot biology: Literature overview and field studies in the Lippe Floodplain (North Rhine-Westphalia)
AU  - BunzeI-Drüke, M.
AU  - Scharf, M.
AU  - Zimball, O.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - An overview of the burbot's biology was compiled from literature. Bottleneck in the life cycle of most burbot populations seems to be the larval stage - a fact that has been largely overlooked so far. Apparently, the larval habitats in Central Europe were floodplains continuously inundated in the spring. The range of the burbot at the Middle Lippe River consists of a section of the floodplain just 40 km long, and it is one of the last populations in North Rhine-Westphalia, comprising only some hundred individuals. Burbot seem to spawn here in early January in small tributaries. The larvae hatch in February or March. Floods in March seem to be especially favourable for the larval development. The burbot's reproduction rate in 2000 was exceptional; in the spring of that year a section of river valley called 'Klostermersch', restored in 1997, had been inundated for 35 days. In 2000 the 'Klostermersch' represented the centre of distribution of young-of-the-year burbot in the Lippe River. Most important conservation measure for the burbot in North Rhine-Westphalia is the restoration of the unity of rivers and floodplains.
