T2  - Natur und Landschaft
AB  - The 900-hectare Meißendorfer Teiche/Bannetzer Moor site has been a designated nature conservation area since 1984. The article reports on the development of dragonflies from 1983 to 2015, especially in the approximately 400-hectare area of the former fishponds. Since this is an artificial pond area, management for preservation was necessary. Because of the large number of ponds, it was possible to carry out various measures, which resulted in an increasingly diverse array of different pond types. A total of 51 dragonfly species has been recorded. The overall number of autochthonous species has increased while only one species has disappeared. Several new species have colonised the site and have reproduced successfully. The findings show that the extensive and diverse uses of the ponds have delivered positive conservation outcomes.
