AB  - The study examines the FFH site proposals in the Federal State of North Rhine-Westphalia (NW) to the European network of conservation areas 'Natura 2000'. After introducing faunistic characteristics and investigation methods of the sites the paper illustrates why the 490 FFH site proposals and the 15 International Bird Areas are assessed as insufficient from a nature conservation point of view as regards the insects according to Appendix II of the Habitats Directive (34 species in Germany. II in NW). The areas were determined by the state government of NW in 2000 and forwarded in spring 2001 to the Federal Ministry of the Environment. The nature conservation administrations take on an important new role during the implementation process of the FFH Impact Assessment and for the determination of the FFH sites. Central instrument to implement the aims of 'Natura 2000' on the level of counties in NW is the landscape plan. The present scientific knowledge of the distribution and ecology of the species needs urgent improvement as well as the quality of the data, which are partly difficult to access. The Federal Agency for Nature Conservation (Bundesamt für Naturschutz) and the responsible agencies in the Federal States are developing first forward-looking projects of bring together the nature conservation activities for the forthcoming tasks, in particular the surveillance of the conservation state of the FFH sites.
