TY  - JOUR
TI  - The effectiveness of measures to promote amphibians in the agricultural areas of Schleswig-Holstein - protection of amphibians by extensive grazing of stream banks and re-wetting measures
AU  - Grell, H.
AU  - Grell, O.
AU  - Voss, K.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - In the north and east of Schleswig-Holstein 120 water bodies, having been created for the promotion of amphibians, were investigated in detail from April to August 1997. Nine amphibian species were found in total with an average figure of 2.4 and a maximum figure of 7 amphibians per water body. Non-endangered amphibians were considerably promoted by the establishment of the water bodies. Of the endangered species, the Great Crested Newt benefited the most. However, the hitherto customary, excavated ponds did not prove very suitable for the retention of the highly endangered amphibians. Fencing results in undisturbed succession and hence in the silting up of the lakes leading to undesirable shading of the water. Within 10 to 15 years these lakes are hardly suited anymore as spawning habitats for amphibians. Several large-scale damming-measures, in particular those with extensive variable water level zones, exhibited considerably higher species and individual counts than those of the excavated ponds. The results suggest a rework of the existing support programmes for amphibians. It is recommended to particularly promote large-scale damming measures with extensive variable water level zones. Preferably, the measures should be located in close distance to water bodies accommodating target species. Integration of the measures into semi-open pasture landscapes would ensure their sustainability.
