TY  - JOUR
TI  - The oligochaeta (annelida) of a near-natural lowland stream in northern Germany
AU  - Löhlein, B.
AU  - Böttger, K.
T2  - Limnologica
AB  - Knowledge of the Oligochaeta fauna of lowland streams is scarce. For the present study on these often neglected animals we chose the "Unterer Schierenseebach" (Lower Schierensee Brook), a small northern German lake outlet, which has previously been shown to be inhabited by many invertebrate species characteristic of near-natural conditions. Despite the fact that the stream is only 800 m long, it offers a broad variety of bottom substrate types ranging from gravel to silt. The five prevailing substrate types cover substrates poor in organic content (sand, gravel), substrates rich in organic content (broken mussle shells, silty sand) and one dominated by organic material (silt). Quantitative sampling of Oligochaeta was carried out at monthly intervals from May to December, 1991, using a corer. A total of 34 taxa of Oligochaeta and Aphanoneura were found. Substrate preferences of the frequent species are characterized. Although many species were found in all substrates, most Tubificidae and the Naididae Nais pardalis, Uncinais uncinata, Specaria josinae preferred the organically enriched substrates, whereas others (Eiaeniella tetraedra, Stylodrilus heringianus, Pristina aequiseta f. foreli, Pristinella aff. jenkinae, Cernosvitoviella sp.) were more abundant in substrates poor in organic material. Bothrioneurum vejdovskyanum (Tubificidae) and Nais behningi (Naididae) were found only in gravel. The occurrence of both these two species and Cernosvitoviella sp. (Enchytraeidae) appears to depend on substrates with a low organic content. The relatively high number of 34 taxa found can be interpreted as an effect of the diverse bottom substrate structures, since no single substrate type was inhabited by more than 25 species. The assemblages of Oligochaeta in the various substrates studied differed considerably in terms of species number (7-25), diversity (Hs = 0.59-2.28, E = 0.36-0.75), density (2,700-12,500 ind./ m2) and species composition, but substrates of similar organic content were inhabited by nearly identical assemblages, suggesting that the organic content of the substrates was the main factor structuring these assemblages. The low oligochaete densities found could be characteristic of undisturbed lowland streams.
