TY  - JOUR
TI  - The substrate-specific distribution of water mites (Hydrachnidia, Acari) in two streams of the North German Lowland (Osterau und Rodenbek, Schleswig-Holstein)
AU  - Martin, P.
AU  - Speth, S.
T2  - Limnologica
AB  - We recorded the substrate-specific distribution of water mites (Hydrachnidia) in sandy reaches of the North German Lowland stream Osterau and its tributary Rodenbek on seven different substrate types: alder roots (Phytal A), Ranunculus-slems (Phytal R); Berula erecta (Phytal B), dead leaves and wood (Macropelal), fine paniculate organic matter (Micropelal), sand (Psammal) and gravel (Akal). A total of 24 species of water mites (Rodenbek: 13, Osterau: 17) was recorded. The highest species richness (9 species) was found on fine paniculate organic matter (Rodenbek) and on alder roots (11 species, Osterau). The lowest species richness was found in both streams on sand (Rodenbek: 3 species, Osterau: 4 species). The highest mean densities (adults and nymphs) were found on Berula erecta (Rodenbek: 105,5 Ind./0,1 m2) and on alder roots (Osterau: 79,8 Ind./0,1 m2). The lowest mean densities were found on sand (Rodenbek: <1 Ind./0,1 m2, Osterau: 1,2 Ind./0,1 m2). Observations showed that within sandy stream channels 5 (Sperchon clupeifer, S. setiger, S. turgidus, Lebertia rivulorum, Atractides nodipalpis) of the 7 most abundant species in the Osterau showed a marked preference (Index of representation) for alder roots. Hygrobates fluviatilis showed a preference for Ranunculus-stems and Berula erecta, Hygrobates nigromaculatus for fine paniculate organic matter. None of the recorded species showed a preference for mineral substrates (sand, gravel). The most abundant species of the Rodenbek (Hygrobates fluviatilis) showed a marked preference for Berula erecta. Results from these field studies indicate that within sandy stream channel an undisturbed stream biocoenosis depends on organic substrates such as alder roots, Berula erecta, Ranunculus-stems, dead leaves and wood and fine particulate organic matter.
