TI  - The Rhine alluvium at Dusseldorf-Urdenbach and its actual importance to landscale, nature and species conservation in the lower Rhine region
AU  - Verbucheln, G.
AU  - Hubinger, A.
T2  - Acta Biologica Benrodis
AB  - The Rhine alluvium at Dusseldorf-Urdenbach contains in total 27 plant species, which are endangered according the "Red List of NRW', among them Euphorbia palustris and Senecio paludosus, both strongly endangered. Due to periodical inundations combined with partially extensive agriculture a great number of vegetation units typical for river alluvia has developed. Some of these, especially the Sanguisorbo-Silaetum and the Arrhenatheretum elatioris, occupy an extensive area. Relict stands of the Querco-Ulmetum and the Salicetum albae have to be considered as supra-regional rarities. 21% of all registered breeding birds are listed in the "Red List NRW'. The characteristic distribution of breeding birds related to structural defined biotope elements (Old Rhine with reed, alluvial meadows, hardwood-/softwood forests) suggests bird communities typical for river alluvia. The entire area is worthy of conservation. -from English summary
